"""
## Purpose and audience
Deliberate-fail probe for the engineers who own the sspec runner and the
scoring lanes: a single scenario whose only assertion is guaranteed RED, used
to prove the runner actually reports outcome=ERROR with a nonzero failed
count instead of silently passing an unrunnable file.
## Operator workflow
Run
`./src/compiler_rust/target/bootstrap/simple run test/01_unit/compiler/r2_probe_fail_spec.spl`
from the repo root and require `outcome=ERROR ... failed=1`. The probe must
stay RED; a green run means the runner stopped executing assertions.
## Compatibility and limitations
The probe compares two literals on purpose — it must fail for a reason no
product change can repair, so it cannot use real product calls and it can
never score as a healthy spec. It is a runner-health fixture, not feature
coverage.
## Verification guidance and troubleshooting
If the run reports `outcome=OK` or `failed=0`, the runner's assertion
execution is broken (or the scenario was weakened — restore
`expect(1).to_equal(2)` verbatim). If the file fails to load, the probe has
drifted from current syntax and must be repaired, not deleted.
"""
# @req REQ-SSPEC-COMPILER
# research: doc/01_research/domain/sspec_documentization_maintenance.md ; plan: doc/03_plan/sspec_modernization_plan.md ; architecture: doc/04_architecture/sspec_documentization_maintenance.md ; design: doc/05_design/infra/sspec/modern_sspec_typed_evidence_design.md
# @manual_section: runner-red-probe



use std.spec.step
use std.io_runtime.{file_read}

describe "R2 deliberate fail probe":
    # @manual scenario evidence
    it "must report RED":
        # @req REQ-SSPEC-COMPILER
        # @capture(statistics): the runner's failed=1 verdict for this scenario is the captured evidence
        step("must report RED")
        val probe_source = file_read("test/01_unit/compiler/r2_probe_fail_spec.spl")
        val keeps_deliberate_red_assertion = probe_source.contains("expect(1).to_equal(2)")
        expect(keeps_deliberate_red_assertion).to_equal(true)  # oracle: the probe is only valid while its deliberate RED literal is verbatim in the file
        expect(1).to_equal(2)  # oracle: 2 is deliberately not 1 — this probe exists to fail and must never be "fixed"
